  <p class="eyebrow">The Researcher</p>
  <h1>Samantha Seligman-Grajewski</h1>
  <p class="lede">Third generation. Granddaughter of Helen Land&oacute; Helman of Tiszabogd&aacute;ny. Founder of Engineering Remembrance.</p>

  <p>I work in process engineering and I am finishing an M.S. in Chemical Engineering at Stanford. I had no training in Holocaust research or genealogy when I started, and I still have none. What I had was a way of thinking about systems, and a grandmother whose family had been reduced to a village name and a handful of first names.</p>

  <p>Helen was the only survivor of her immediate family. Her mother, her father and five of her brothers and sisters were murdered. For most of my life we had nothing of them. No photograph, no grave, no date. The family held one Buchenwald prisoner card for her father and assumed that was the end of what could be known.</p>

  <p>I started looking properly in 2026, and I have not stopped since.</p>

  <h2>What I found</h2>

  <p>Among the records are the Buchenwald intake photographs of Helen&rsquo;s father and his two nephews, carters like him, photographed one behind the other and numbered 322, 323 and 324. They were the first images our family had ever seen of anyone on Helen&rsquo;s side.</p>

  <p>Through records alone I reconstructed the nephews&rsquo; survival: both lived, and in June 1945 they found Helen in Budapest and told her what had happened to her family. She described those two men in her own recorded testimony and never named them. The documents named them.</p>

  <p>Following them forward reached living descendants in Israel and the United States, from a branch that had settled on a kibbutz before the war. Their family tree was missing Zacharias and David, two of Iczik&rsquo;s sons, and everyone who came after them. I was able to tell them that one of those brothers had a granddaughter who survived.</p>

  <h2>A future prediction she left</h2>

  <p>In 2008 Helen sat for a recorded interview. Near the end, the interviewer asks about her grandchildren.</p>

  <p>I was sixteen. I had said once, at her kitchen table, that I wanted to go to Stanford, and then I never applied. I went to UCLA for my undergraduate degree. Eighteen years later I applied to Stanford for a master&rsquo;s and was admitted, and that July I listened to this tape again and heard what she had done with a passing remark from a teenager.</p>

